# C++ Low-Latency Rules

- Use C++20 and make the smallest complete change.
- Preserve observable behavior and existing module boundaries unless explicitly asked otherwise.
- Prefer RAII and explicit ownership; avoid raw owning pointers.
- Make ownership, synchronization, lifetime, and atomic ordering explicit.
- Do not introduce data races or silently ignore failures.
- Do not introduce fallback, retry, or silent recovery unless explicitly required.
- Do not refactor unrelated code or add speculative abstractions.
- In low-latency paths, avoid allocation, blocking calls, unnecessary copies, unnecessary virtual dispatch, and exception-based control flow.
- Preserve cache locality and avoid false sharing where relevant.
- Do not claim performance improvements without measurement or a clear mechanical reason.